@ahg-g 

> Why not just insert the unschedulable plugin to pInfo.UnschedulablePlugins if preCheck fails here

Two reasons in my mind when I implemented c967331ecedc017e9cee74b8809fa15ff0ea53b6:
- We want to insert the plugin name from preCheck **only when** `event` is interesting for some `pInfo.UnschedulablePlugins`.
  - Otherwise, it'd result in a regression. For example, every time an unready node event comes, all Pods would get `TaintToleration` in `pInfo.UnschedulablePlugins`, even though some of them may be waiting for only Pod events to arrive.
- `Pending` status handling; we should run preCheck after Pending plugins are executed.
  - Otherwise, if some of `pInfo.PendingPlugins` are waiting for some Node events but preCheck filters Node events out, it might result in Pods being stuck forever at unschedQ.
  - (Note that, this is a problem for the current master branch too, so basically, it aims to solve an additional existing problem.)

If we want to do it in moveAllToActiveOrBackoffQueue, it'd be like

```go
func (p *PriorityQueue) moveAllToActiveOrBackoffQueue(logger klog.Logger, event framework.ClusterEvent, oldObj, newObj interface{}, preCheck PreEnqueueCheck) {
	if !p.isEventOfInterest(logger, event) {
		// No plugin is interested in this event.
		// Return early before iterating all pods in unschedulablePods for preCheck.
		return
	}
	
	unschedulablePods := make([]*framework.QueuedPodInfo, 0, len(p.unschedulablePods.podInfoMap))
	for _, pInfo := range p.unschedulablePods.podInfoMap {
		if !interestToUnschedulablePlugins(pInfo, event) { continue } // This Pod isn't interested in this event, ignore.

		if preCheck != nil {
			if admissionResult := preCheck(pInfo.Pod); admissionResult != nil {
				if admissionResult.Name != "" {
					// If a plugin rejects a Pod in preCheck, we should treat the pod as rejected by the plugin.
					// Here, we may register non-registered plugins to pInfo.UnschedulablePlugins if a user uses a custom scheduler configuration though,
					// we don't have to care about it because it's just ignored.
					pInfo.UnschedulablePlugins.Insert(admissionResult.Name)
				}
				continue // This event is filtered out by preCheck, ignore.
			}
		}

		unschedulablePods = append(unschedulablePods, pInfo) 
	}
	p.movePodsToActiveOrBackoffQueue(logger, unschedulablePods, event, oldObj, newObj)
}

func interestToUnschedulablePlugins(pInfo, event) bool {
	rejectorPlugins := pInfo.UnschedulablePlugins.Union(pInfo.PendingPlugins)

	for eventToMatch, hintfns := range p.queueingHintMap[pInfo.Pod.Spec.SchedulerName] {
		if !eventToMatch.Match(event) {
			continue
		}

		for _, hintfn := range hintfns {
			if !rejectorPlugins.Has(hintfn.PluginName) {
				// skip if it's not hintfn from rejectorPlugins.
				continue
			}
			return true // some pInfo.UnschedulablePlugins are interested in this event.
		}
	}
	return false // no pInfo.UnschedulablePlugins are interested in this event.
}

```

But, I'm not sure which is better; I personally prefer the original way https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/commit/c967331ecedc017e9cee74b8809fa15ff0ea53b6 since https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/commit/c967331ecedc017e9cee74b8809fa15ff0ea53b6 addresses Pending plugin issue as well and require no additional `for` loop.

@ahg-g I changed the implementation for https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/pull/122292#issuecomment-1979894913 's direction then as I don't have a strong opinion; 
- As noted in the comment, the issue with PreCheck/Pending plugin is a separate issue, and I believe no in-tree plugin is impacted by this bug for now anyway. 
- Then I'd rather take the readability of the implementation for everyone by having additional `for`, which would be a big deal for overall efficiency.

